    "q-11481": {
      "dom_id": "step-q11481",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14238267/assets/63bc2b3795556cd1f1b3b5b63c8d92816f64edcad364664db25acc0a7cc59b9e.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?",
      "worked_solution": "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=3.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-11481-a",
              "type": "math",
