    "q-139201": {
      "dom_id": "step-q139201",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14238267/assets/780120c106065874c04d97b8b6ef10e3e81e1cf9fd7c7b19bc633963cbadafe4.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=4x-2{x}^{2}$ and $y=2{x}^{3},$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $f(x)=4x-2{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=2{x}^{3}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 4x-2{x}^{2} & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ 4x-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ -2x(-2+x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$  and $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1$ because we are only concerned about $x\\ge 0.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14238267/assets/e695cfd83116df98b1530e2d53186de0ebcd03c6056c2b8d7c2272a76fbd790c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=4x-2{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x([4x-2{x}^{2}]-[2{x}^{3}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(4x-2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
