      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14238267/assets/a9db1a85c150c0bd6a8913a19387c7c717103cff1faf5266c7e21e774ab193ab.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=-{x}^{2}+2x+1$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+1.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x+1 & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ -2{x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ -2x(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0,$ and $x=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=1.$  \n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14238267/assets/8790ae667c9a2741e2a732ff69d9afe5633dc26df7b02eee52fb51478524282b.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x([-{x}^{2}+2x+1]-[{x}^{2}+1])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2x-2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
